{"SurveyID":"SV_5szpmzBE6gxqTwq","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID3611","SecondaryAttribute":" ","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"&nbsp;","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"attChk.severity","QuestionType":"Matrix","Selector":"Likert","SubSelector":"SingleAnswer","Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText","TextPosition":"inline","ChoiceColumnWidth":25,"RepeatHeaders":"none","WhiteSpace":"OFF","MobileFirst":true},"QuestionDescription":" ","Choices":{"1":{"Display":"<p style=\"min-width:350px;\">Which do you find more horrific, dying like the people in Ankradem, or dying like the people in Ankradem?<br><\/p>"}},"ChoiceOrder":["1"],"Validation":{"Settings":{"ForceResponse":"OFF","Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":5,"NextAnswerId":9,"Answers":{"1":{"Display":"1: Dying like people in Ankradem"},"6":{"Display":"2"},"2":{"Display":"3"},"3":{"Display":"4"},"4":{"Display":"5"},"5":{"Display":"6: Dying like people in Ankradem"}},"AnswerOrder":["1","6","2","3","4","5"],"ChoiceDataExportTags":false,"QuestionID":"QID3611","DataVisibility":{"Private":false,"Hidden":false},"RecodeValues":{"1":"1","2":"3","3":"4","4":"5","5":"6","6":"2"}}},
{"SurveyID":"SV_5szpmzBE6gxqTwq","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID361","SecondaryAttribute":"!!! TO GET PAID, PLEASE CONTINUE TO THE NEXT\u00a0 QUESTION EVEN IF YOU DON'T WISH TO READ THIS PARAGR..","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><span style=\"color:#c0392b;\"><span style=\"font-size:22px;\"><strong>!!! TO GET PAID, PLEASE CONTINUE TO THE NEXT&nbsp; QUESTION EVEN IF YOU DON&#39;T WISH TO READ THIS PARAGRAPH !!!<\/strong><\/span><\/span><\/p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>What this study is about<\/strong><\/p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;<\/div>\n\n<div>Thanks so much for taking part in this study!<\/div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;<\/div>\n\n<div>We ran this survey to better understand how people make decisions when facing moral dilemmas where some persons can only be saved by &ldquo;sacrificing&rdquo; others. Scientists and philosophers disagree about the basis of such decisions. Some propose that individuals approach moral dilemmas with a simple mindset and consider any kind of harm as bad, which precludes sacrificing people for the greater good. Others propose that individuals maximize the amount of lives that are saved rather than sacrificed.<\/div>\n<br \/>\nHere, we test an alternative view drawing on how people generally compare other quantities, from numbers to sound intensities to weights: The greater the relative difference, the easier it is to compare the quantities. For example, telling apart a 100 gram weight from a 200 gram weight is easy, because the relative difference is 100%; telling apart a 10 kg weight for a 10.1 kg weight is hard because the relative difference is only 1%, even though the differences is the same (i.e., 100 grams). In our study, we would like to know whether respondents refrain from sacrificing others, whether they try to maximize the number of survivors &ndash; or whether the acceptability of sacrificing others depends on the relative differences between the survivors and the sacrificed. Additionally, we are curious whether respondents approach such dilemmas differently depending on if it involves economic loss rather than loss of life.\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>","DefaultChoices":false,"DataExportTag":"debrief","QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","Configuration":{"QuestionDescriptionOption":"UseText"},"QuestionDescription":"!!! TO GET PAID, PLEASE CONTINUE TO THE NEXT\u00a0 QUESTION EVEN IF YOU DON'T WISH TO READ THIS PARAGR...","ChoiceOrder":[],"Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"GradingData":[],"Language":[],"NextChoiceId":4,"NextAnswerId":1,"QuestionID":"QID361"}},
{"SurveyID":"SV_5szpmzBE6gxqTwq","Element":"QC","PrimaryAttribute":"Survey Question Count","SecondaryAttribute":"5772","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":null},
{"SurveyID":"SV_5szpmzBE6gxqTwq","Element":"SQ","PrimaryAttribute":"QID3619","SecondaryAttribute":"Aqueduct in the desert Settlements in the Burning Barren desert are supplied with water through a...","TertiaryAttribute":null,"Payload":{"QuestionText":"<b>Aqueduct in the desert<\/b><br><br>\n\nSettlements in the Burning Barren desert are supplied with water through a series of aqueducts. The supply to Sandharbor settlement has been poisoned with Possuxyth, a potent, fast acting but relatively pain-free poison.\n<br> <br>\nAll of the people in Sandharbor will die if nothing is done. There are few options for relief efforts, as there are no off-road tanker trucks and any evacuation would take too much time because both communities are remote and inaccessible. However, the control engineer can remotely switch the supply to the Midcross settlement to Sandharbor. This would dilute the poison enough to make the water safe to drink, but kill all of the people in Midcross.\n<br> <br>\nYou are the control engineer. If you switch the supply from Midcross to Sandharbor, you will save all of the 4,000 people in Sandharbor, but all of the 40 people in Midcross will endure a painful death due to dehydration, which can take up to a week.<br><br>\n\n","DataExportTag":"scenario.desertAqueduct.vivid.counterbalancing_group.1.n_saved.4000.n_total.4040.n_victims.40.ratio_saved_victims.100.text","QuestionID":"QID3619","QuestionType":"DB","Selector":"TB","QuestionDescription":"Aqueduct in the desert Settlements in the Burning Barren desert are supplied with water through a...","Validation":{"Settings":{"Type":"None"}},"Language":[]}},
